title: Multi-origin batches paid by their submitter
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    This PR adds `pallet-utility-ext`, which allows batching calls with different origins in one
    signed transaction. The origins can be custom, they don't have to be
    `SystemOrigin::Signed(AccountId)`. This is done by supplying a number of batch items
    consisting of a call, its inner `TransactionExtension` pipeline and its expected origin,
    along with the submitter's nonce, to `pallet_utility_ext::batch_multi_origin` or
    `force_batch_multi_origin`.

    The batch is a regular signed transaction of its submitter. Its call applies the items in
    sequence: each item runs its inner pipeline's `validate` and `prepare`, is dispatched and runs
    its pipeline's `post_dispatch` before the next item starts, as if it were a transaction of
    its own included right after the previous item. An invalid item is not applied,
    like an invalid transaction; an item whose call fails keeps its pipeline's effects (nonce)
    while the call's effects are reverted, like a failed transaction. Batches are not atomic:
    `batch_multi_origin` stops at the first item which is invalid or fails and keeps the items
    before it, `force_batch_multi_origin` applies every item regardless. Items are not validated
    in the transaction pool, only the submitter's transaction is.

    The submitter pays for the whole batch like for any transaction: the overhead, the items'
    pipelines and calls and the transaction's length. Items pay nothing. The weight and bytes of
    items whose dispatch returns `Pays::No` are refunded to the submitter. Items are signed over an
    implication binding them to the submitter, the submitter's nonce, the batch mode, their
    position and the other items, so an item cannot be applied elsewhere. The call checks that
    `nonce + 1` is the submitter's current nonce, i.e. that `nonce` is the one `CheckNonce` just
    bumped.

    Enabling this feature means configuring `pallet-utility-ext` in your runtime. Importantly, the
    pallet requires:

    - A maximum number of calls allowed in a single batch. This should generally be a low figure
      (e.g. 8 calls).
    - A call filter for which calls are allowed to be part of multi origin batches.
    - The inner `TransactionExtension` pipeline carried by each item. This is different than the
      runtime's overarching `TransactionExtension` and is meant to be a subset of the extensions
      there, with the signature verification first and without the fee payment extension and the
      extensions which are supposed to run only once per transaction (e.g. `CheckWeight`,
      `WeightReclaim`). With the `runtime-benchmarks` feature, a benchmark helper building items
      of that type is required as well.

    The pallet's `MultiOriginBatch` transaction extension wraps the runtime's fee payment
    extension in its slot of the overarching `TransactionExtension`. The fee payment extension charges the
    submitter for the batch like for any transaction, and the wrapper makes it settle the fee
    without the weight and bytes of the items whose dispatch returned `Pays::No`, leaving the
    weight the block accounts for unchanged. Calls relying on the transient fee credit of
    `pallet-transaction-payment` belonging to the transaction's signer, today the Ethereum
    transaction calls of `pallet_revive`, must be kept out of batches by the call filter.

    For wallets, the item pipeline type is part of the pallet's metadata and the encoding of the
    payload an item's origin authorizes is documented on `ItemImplication`.
    `TransactionValidityError` and its variants now derive `DecodeWithMemTracking`.
crates:
- name: pallet-utility-ext
  bump: major
- name: sp-runtime
  bump: minor
- name: polkadot-sdk
  bump: minor
